     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	# Slice-0 artifact gates (PROVISIONAL, Concrete/README.md gates 10, 11 and 12), over every
     3	# hand-written BIR example, on the executables slice0_native.sh links:
     4	#
     5	# 10. Sanitizers: the LLVM example also built with ASan (slice0_native.sh sanitize: ASan
     6	#     instruments the printed IR once every definition carries sanitize_address; no UBSan: its
     7	#     checks are inserted by clang's C front end, so on IR input it checks nothing) must
     8	#     give the same observation as the BIR semantics (status, exact fd-2 bytes, empty fd 1), so no
     9	#     sanitizer report. Its memory is bounded by ASan's hard_rss_limit_mb (the shadow reservation
    10	#     rules out an address-space cap; every other command is capped, see SLICE0_GATE_MEM_GB). Control: a body with an out-of-bounds load must be reported.
    11	# 11. Binary size (report-only, never fails on a size): bytes and text size of each executable,
    12	#     per backend and target, in REPORT/sizes.tsv. Compile time (report-only, owner 2026-11-08):
    13	#     wall-clock milliseconds of each example's emit step (the Lean emitter: checks, LLVM print,
    14	#     LCB adaptation and in-process assembly, Lean start-up included) and native step (clang and
    15	#     LCB's command line, linking both executables), next to its budget (the step's time bound), in
    16	#     REPORT/compile_times.tsv.
    17	# 12. Stack bound: lib/stack_bound.py derives, from the linked executable's disassembly, a bound on
    18	#     the Concrete-owned stack below main's entry, covering every byte addressed relative to the
    19	#     stack or frame pointer (so the red zone), with external libc calls listed with their entry
    20	#     depth (their own use, and that _exit and abort do not return, are premises). A run under
    21	#     gdb (x86-64 Linux, lib/stack_measure.py) or LLDB (aarch64-macos, lib/stack_measure_lldb.py)
    22	#     must stay within it (the run measures the stack pointer only). REPORT/stack.tsv. Controls: the analysis must refuse an unmodelled
    23	#     stack-pointer write, an indirect call, recursion, a call to an unknown function, two depths
    24	#     meeting, a stack pointer used as a value or written through a partial register, a partial
    25	#     frame-pointer write, an indexed stack address, a saved frame pointer popped into another
    26	#     register, a stack address taken, an overwritten return address, an AArch64 frame pointer
    27	#     stored, a register-directed return, a return after a call without the link register
    28	#     restored, a freed or restored saved slot reread, a callee's leftover saved slot reread by
    29	#     its caller (both architectures), a read of bytes written on one path only or never written,
    30	#     a store through main's argv, a PC-relative base kept across a call, walked by repeated adds or
    31	#     beyond the reach premise, an absolute address (memory only through sp/fp, PC-relative or fs/gs), a callee that does not restore the frame pointer, a frame record away from sp, an unmodelled
    32	#     instruction (x86 `loop`) and frame- or link-register writeback (AArch64), and give the
    33	#     expected bound on known functions (x86 frame, red-zone store, two AArch64 frame-record
    34	#     shapes, a read of bytes written on both paths, an adrp-based load); the comparison must reject a
    35	#     measurement above the bound.
    36	#
    37	# usage: slice0_artifacts.sh [REPORT_DIR]. Run inside the nix devshell, from the repository root.
    38	set -euo pipefail
    39	report=${1:-}
    40	# The sanitizer executions need an unbounded address-space reservation, so the gate sets no cap of
    41	# its own and caps every other command it starts (`capped`, and limited_exec.sh for executables).
    42	SLICE0_GATE_MEM_GB=0
    43	# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/slice0_common.sh
    44	source scripts/checks/lib/slice0_common.sh
    45	: "${CONCRETE_SLICE0_LLVM:?run inside nix develop}"
    46	objdump=(bounded 60 "$CONCRETE_SLICE0_LLVM/llvm-objdump" -d --no-show-raw-insn)
    47	case "$target" in
    48	  x86_64-linux) arch=x86_64; objdump+=(--x86-asm-syntax=intel) ;;
    49	  aarch64-*) arch=aarch64 ;;
    50	esac
    51	# The run check's debugger: gdb on x86-64 Linux, LLDB on aarch64-macos (none elsewhere).
    52	gdb= lldb=
    53	case "$target" in
    54	  x86_64-linux) gdb=${CONCRETE_SLICE0_GDB:?the x86-64 Linux shell pins gdb} ;;
    55	  aarch64-macos)
    56	    lldb=${CONCRETE_SLICE0_LLDB:?the macOS shell names lldb}
    57	    [ -x "$lldb" ] || { echo "slice0_artifacts: no LLDB at $lldb" >&2; exit 2; }
    58	    echo "lldb: $("$lldb" --version 2>&1 | head -c 200 | head -1)" ;;
    59	esac
    60	measuring=${gdb:-$lldb}
    61	[ -n "$report" ] || report="$work/report"
    62	mkdir -p "$report"
    63	sizes="$report/sizes.tsv"
    64	stack="$report/stack.tsv"
    65	printf 'example\tbackend\ttarget\tbytes\ttext\n' >"$sizes"
    66	times="$report/compile_times.tsv"
    67	printf 'example\ttarget\temit_ms\temit_budget_ms\tnative_ms\tnative_budget_ms\n' >"$times"
    68	# now_ms: a monotonic clock in milliseconds (portable: BSD date has no %N).
    69	now_ms() { python3 -I -c 'import time; print(time.monotonic_ns() // 1000000)'; }
    70	printf 'example\tbackend\ttarget\tbound\tmeasured\texternals\n' >"$stack"
    71	# hard_rss_limit_mb and LeakSanitizer are Linux-only (ASan on Darwin refuses detect_leaks); on
    72	# macOS RLIMIT_AS is not enforced either, so there the job's memory is the bound, as for all runs.
    73	asan=abort_on_error=0:symbolize=0
    74	[ "$(uname -s)" != Linux ] || asan="$asan:hard_rss_limit_mb=1024:detect_leaks=1"
    75	san_env=("ASAN_OPTIONS=$asan")
    76	# stack_bound ARCH DISASM [--ranges]: the analysis, capped and bounded in time.
    77	stack_bound() { bounded 60 python3 -I "$root/scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py" "$@"; }
    78	fail=0
    79	examples=0
    80	last_exe= last_analysis=
    81	
    82	# within MEASURED BOUND: the run stayed within the static bound (and actually ran).
    83	within() { [ "$1" -gt 0 ] && [ "$1" -le "$2" ]; }
    84	
    85	# measure EXE ANALYSIS EXPECT: the run check's output (`measured N` or `error ...`), given the
    86	# analysis output (its `range` lines) and the expected exit status: gdb (lib/stack_measure.py) or
    87	# LLDB (lib/stack_measure_lldb.py, the same contract; Apple's LLDB runs its own Python, so the
    88	# shell's Python variables are removed).
    89	measure() {
    90	  local ranges main
    91	  ranges=$(awk '$1 == "range" {printf "%s%s:%s", sep, $3, $4; sep=","}' <<<"$2")
    92	  main=$(awk '$1 == "range" && $2 == "main" {print $3}' <<<"$2")
    93	  if [ -n "$gdb" ]; then
    94	    STACK_RANGES=$ranges STACK_MAIN=$main STACK_EXPECT=$3 \
    95	      bounded 300 "$gdb" -batch -nx -x "$root/scripts/checks/lib/stack_measure.py" \
    96	      "$1" 2>"$1.gdb.err" | awk '$1 == "measured" || $1 == "error" {print}' | cap_out || true
    97	  else
    98	    STACK_EXE=$1 STACK_RANGES=$ranges STACK_MAIN=$main STACK_EXPECT=$3 \
    99	      bounded 300 env -u PYTHONPATH -u PYTHONHOME "$lldb" --batch --no-lldbinit \
   100	      -o "command script import $root/scripts/checks/lib/stack_measure_lldb.py" \
   101	      2>"$1.gdb.err" | awk '$1 == "measured" || $1 == "error" {print}' | cap_out || true
   102	  fi
   103	}
   104	
   105	# stack_check NAME BACKEND EXE: derive the bound, measure a run where supported, record both.
   106	stack_check() {
   107	  local name=$1 backend=$2 exe=$3 dis="$3.dis" out bound ext m measured=not-run
   108	  if ! "${objdump[@]}" "$exe" >"$dis" 2>"$dis.err"; then
   109	    echo "FAIL $name [$backend] stack: disassembly failed: $(head -c 300 "$dis.err")"; fail=1; return
   110	  fi
   111	  if ! out=$(stack_bound "$arch" "$dis" --ranges | cap_out); then
   112	    echo "FAIL $name [$backend] stack: $out"; fail=1; return
   113	  fi
   114	  bound=$(awk '$1 == "bound" {print $2}' <<<"$out")
   115	  ext=$(awk '$1 == "external" {printf "%s%s@%s", sep, $2, $3; sep=","}' <<<"$out")
   116	  if [ -n "$measuring" ]; then
   117	    m=$(measure "$exe" "$out" "$(head -c 16 "$(dirname "$exe")/status")")
   118	    measured=$(awk '$1 == "measured" {print $2}' <<<"$m")
   119	    if [ -z "$measured" ] || ! within "$measured" "$bound"; then
   120	      echo "FAIL $name [$backend] stack: bound $bound, run: ${m:-no measurement} $(head -c 300 "$exe.gdb.err")"
   121	      fail=1; return
   122	    fi
   123	    last_exe=$exe last_analysis=$out
   124	  fi
   125	  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$name" "$backend" "$target" "$bound" "$measured" "$ext" >>"$stack"
   126	  echo "ok   $name [$backend] stack: bound $bound, measured $measured, externals ${ext:-none}"
   127	}
   128	
   129	size_report() { # size_report NAME BACKEND EXE
   130	  local bytes text
   131	  bytes=$(wc -c <"$3" | tr -d ' ')
   132	  text=$(bounded 60 "$CONCRETE_SLICE0_LLVM/llvm-size" "$3" 2>/dev/null |
   133	    awk 'NR == 2 {print $1}' | cap_out) || true
   134	  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$1" "$2" "$target" "$bytes" "${text:-unavailable}" >>"$sizes"
   135	}
   136	
   137	names=$(emit_examples)
   138	mkdir -p "$work/ctl"
   139	for name in $names; do
   140	  d="$work/ex/$name"
   141	  t0=$(now_ms)
   142	  "${emitter[@]}" emit "$name" "$d" "$target"
   143	  t1=$(now_ms)
   144	  build "$name" "$d/build.log" bounded "$native_secs" bash "$root/scripts/checks/slice0_native.sh" "$d" "$lcb"
   145	  t2=$(now_ms)
# gdb -batch -nx -x stack_measure.py EXE (README gate 12, x86-64 Linux). PROVISIONAL.
#
# Measures the stack depth one complete run of EXE reaches, below the stack pointer at `main`'s
# entry, by single-stepping every instruction of the Concrete-owned functions (STACK_RANGES:
# "LO:HI,..." in the executable's link-time addresses, from stack_bound.py --ranges; STACK_MAIN:
# main's LO). An external function (entered through a call, so its return address is at the stack
# pointer) runs at full speed to that return address; its own use is outside the bound, as in
# stack_bound.py, but the depth at its entry is measured. Once main has returned, the rest runs
# freely.
#
# Fail closed: the measurement counts only for a run that stopped first at main's runtime address,
# stopped after each external call exactly at its return address (or exited inside it), received
# no signal, and exited normally with status STACK_EXPECT. Prints `measured N` and `steps N`, or
# `error ...` (the gate fails on anything but a measurement).
import os

import gdb

LIMIT = 2_000_000
ranges = [tuple(int(x, 16) for x in r.split(":")) for r in os.environ["STACK_RANGES"].split(",")]
main_lo = int(os.environ["STACK_MAIN"], 16)
expect = int(os.environ["STACK_EXPECT"])
state = {"exit": None, "signal": None}


def on_exit(event):
    state["exit"] = event.exit_code if hasattr(event, "exit_code") else "unknown"


def on_stop(event):
    if isinstance(event, gdb.SignalEvent):
        state["signal"] = event.stop_signal


gdb.events.exited.connect(on_exit)
gdb.events.stop.connect(on_stop)
gdb.execute("set pagination off")
gdb.execute("set confirm off")
gdb.execute("set disable-randomization on")


def reg(name):
    return int(gdb.parse_and_eval(f"(unsigned long) ${name}"))


def live():
    if state["signal"] is not None:
        raise RuntimeError(f"signal {state['signal']}")
    return state["exit"] is None


try:
    gdb.execute("break *main", to_string=True)
    gdb.execute("run </dev/null >/dev/null 2>/dev/null", to_string=True)
    if not live():
        raise RuntimeError("exited before main")
    main_rt = int(gdb.parse_and_eval("(unsigned long) &main"))
    if reg("pc") != main_rt:
        raise RuntimeError(f"first stop at {reg('pc'):#x}, not main ({main_rt:#x})")
    offset = main_rt - main_lo
    sp0 = reg("sp")
    low, steps = sp0, 0
    inferior = gdb.selected_inferior()
    gdb.execute("delete", to_string=True)
    while live():
        pc, sp = reg("pc"), reg("sp")
        if sp > sp0:  # main has returned
            gdb.execute("continue", to_string=True)
            continue
        low = min(low, sp)
        if any(lo + offset <= pc <= hi + offset for lo, hi in ranges):
            gdb.execute("stepi", to_string=True)
            steps += 1
            if steps > LIMIT:
                raise RuntimeError(f"more than {LIMIT} steps")
        else:
            ret = int.from_bytes(inferior.read_memory(sp, 8).tobytes(), "little")
            if not any(lo + offset <= ret <= hi + offset for lo, hi in ranges):
                raise RuntimeError(f"external code at {pc:#x} returns to {ret:#x}, outside the owned code")
            gdb.execute(f"tbreak *{ret:#x}", to_string=True)
            gdb.execute("continue", to_string=True)
            if live() and reg("pc") != ret:
                raise RuntimeError(f"stopped at {reg('pc'):#x}, not the return address {ret:#x}")
    if state["exit"] != expect:
        raise RuntimeError(f"exit status {state['exit']}, expected {expect}")
    if steps == 0:
        raise RuntimeError("no instruction stepped")
    print(f"measured {sp0 - low}")
    print(f"steps {steps}")
except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - every failure is reported, the gate fails closed
    print(f"error {e}")

exec
/bin/bash -lc "command -v lldb; sed -n '190,240p' Concrete/Backend/LCB.lean; cat scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean | head -120; sed -n '400,450p' Concrete/README.md; sed -n '570,595p' Concrete/README.md; cat scripts/checks/slice0_repro.sh; rg 'LCB\\.(adapt|assemble|artifact|emit)|timeout' Concrete scripts/checks/slice0* scripts/checks/lib/slice0*" in /tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/owner-review1
 succeeded in 0ms:
           valueWidths := Array.replicate (valueCount p) .word, blocks := blocks.toArray }

/-- The LCB backend's emit: the adapted body paired with the program's site table (the table that
decodes the body's result is part of the emitted artifact), or the adapter's refusal, in which case
no artifact exists (`artifact` and the emitter build only from `.ok`). `Proof.emit_correct` and
`Proof.artifact_correct` are the backend contract: no premise. -/
def emit (p : Program) : Except String (Source.Function × SiteTable) := do
  let f ← adapt p
  return (f, p.sites)

/-! ## Assembly, in process (inside `P_toolchain[lcb]`) -/

/-- The targets the gate runs LCB's output on. -/
inductive Target where
  | x86_64Linux | aarch64Linux | aarch64Macos
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def Target.ofName : String → Option Target
  | "x86_64-linux" => some .x86_64Linux
  | "aarch64-linux" => some .aarch64Linux
  | "aarch64-macos" => some .aarch64Macos
  | _ => none

/-- The body's symbol: the runtime entry calls `concrete_body`. -/
def bodySymbol : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Symbol := { namespaceName := "concrete", name := "concrete_body", exported := true }

/-- The adapted body as assembly text for `target`, computed in process from the value. -/
def assemble (target : Target) (f : Source.Function) : Except String String :=
  match target with
  | .aarch64Macos => do
    let job : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.FunctionJob :=
      { code := { symbol := bodySymbol, body := f, abi := LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.planAbi f,
                  layout := LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.planFrame f } }
    let artifact ← (LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.compileFunction job).mapError (fun e => s!"LCB first path: {repr e}")
    return ".text\n" ++ artifact.code.fragment ++ ".subsections_via_symbols\n"
  | .x86_64Linux | .aarch64Linux => do
    let m : LeanCompilerBackend.Module :=
      { functions := #[LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.reify f bodySymbol.name bodySymbol.exported] }
    LeanCompilerBackend.validateModule m
    match target with
    | .x86_64Linux => LeanCompilerBackend.AMD64.emitModuleValidated m false false
    | _ => LeanCompilerBackend.AArch64.emitModuleValidated m false false

/-- The LCB body artifact for `target`: the assembly of a successful `emit`, or its refusal. -/
def artifact (target : Target) (p : Program) : Except String String := do
  let (f, _) ← emit p
  assemble target f

/-! ## `adapter_correct`, value level

The first layer of the adapter proof: each BIR instruction's expansion computes, in LCB's 32-bit
import Concrete

/-!
Emitter for the slice-0 backend gate (`scripts/checks/slice0_backends.sh`). PROVISIONAL.

`lake env lean --run scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean list` prints the hand-written BIR examples;
`... emit NAME DIR TARGET` checks the example in Lean (well formed; its BIR run gives the expected
outcome; LCB's own evaluator on the adapted body returns the same result word) and writes
`DIR/llvm.ll` (body + runtime entry), `DIR/lcb_body.s` (the adapted body, assembled by LCB in
process for TARGET: `x86_64-linux`, `aarch64-linux` or `aarch64-macos`), `DIR/lcb_rt.ssa` (the
runtime entry as QBE text, for LCB's command line), `DIR/lcb_body.ssa` (a debug dump only), and the
observation the executables must show: `DIR/status` (the exit status) and `DIR/stderr` (the exact
bytes on descriptor 2; descriptor 1 must stay empty).

Two controls exercise the gate itself: `... loop DIR TARGET` emits a well-formed BIR body that
never returns (`DIR/status` is `incomplete`), and `... invalid DIR TARGET` emits a body returning a
word that encodes nothing, which the runtime must refuse (`Runtime.invalidLine`, then abort).
-/

open Concrete.BIR Concrete.Backend

def writeExpected (dir : System.FilePath) (status : String) (stderr : String) : IO Unit := do
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "status") (status ++ "\n")
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "stderr") stderr

/-- Write the LCB leg: the body assembled in process, the runtime entry as QBE text. -/
def writeLcb (dir : System.FilePath) (target : LCB.Target)
    (emitted : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source.Function × SiteTable) : IO Bool := do
  let (f, sites) := emitted
  IO.FS.createDirAll dir
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "lcb_source.txt") (reprStr f ++ "\n")
  let job : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.FunctionJob :=
    { code := { symbol := LCB.bodySymbol, body := f,
                abi := LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.planAbi f,
                layout := LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.planFrame f } }
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "lcb_job.txt") (reprStr job ++ "\n")
  -- On every host, LCB's verified first path (the `aarch64-macos` target) must admit the body.
  if let .error msg := LCB.assemble .aarch64Macos f then
    IO.eprintln s!"{dir}: LCB first path: {msg}"; return false
  match LCB.assemble target f with
  | .error msg => IO.eprintln s!"{dir}: LCB: {msg}"; return false
  | .ok asm =>
    IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "lcb_body.s") asm
    IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "lcb_rt.ssa") (Runtime.qbe sites)
    IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "lcb_body.ssa") (LCB.render f)
    return true

def writeBackends (dir : System.FilePath) (target : LCB.Target) (p : Program)
    (emitted : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source.Function × SiteTable) : IO Bool := do
  IO.FS.createDirAll dir
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "bir.txt") (reprStr p ++ "\n")
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "llvm.ll") (LLVM.module p)
  writeLcb dir target emitted

def emit (name : String) (dir : System.FilePath) (target : LCB.Target) : IO UInt32 := do
  let some (_, p, expected) := Examples.all.find? (·.1 == name)
    | IO.eprintln s!"unknown example {name}"; return 2
  unless wf p do IO.eprintln s!"{name}: not well formed"; return 1
  let got := run p 1000
  unless got == expected do
    IO.eprintln s!"{name}: BIR run gives {repr got}, expected {repr expected}"; return 1
  let some word := encode expected | IO.eprintln s!"{name}: no result word"; return 1
  match LCB.emit p with
  | .error msg => IO.eprintln s!"{name}: adapter: {msg}"; return 1
  | .ok (f, tbl) =>
    match LCB.evalBody f 100000 with
    | some w =>
      unless w == word do
        IO.eprintln s!"{name}: LCB's evaluator returns {w}, BIR's result word is {word}"; return 1
    | none => IO.eprintln s!"{name}: LCB's evaluator does not return"; return 1
    unless ← writeBackends dir target p (f, tbl) do return 1
    match expected with
    | .exited c => writeExpected dir (toString c) ""
    | .trapped s => match p.sites[s]? with
      | some e => writeExpected dir "134" (Runtime.trapLine e ++ "\n")
      | none => IO.eprintln s!"{name}: no site {s}"; return 1
    | _ => IO.eprintln s!"{name}: no observation"; return 1
    return 0

/-- A body that branches back to itself forever. -/
def loopBody : Program :=
  { blocks := [{ params := [], insts := [], term := .br 1 [] },
               { params := [], insts := [], term := .br 1 [] }], sites := [] }

def emitLoop (dir : System.FilePath) (target : LCB.Target) : IO UInt32 := do
  unless wf loopBody && run loopBody 1000 == .outOfFuel do
    IO.eprintln "loop: not a well-formed diverging body"; return 1
  match LCB.emit loopBody with
  | .error msg => IO.eprintln s!"loop: adapter: {msg}"; return 1
  | .ok (f, tbl) =>
    if (LCB.evalBody f 10000).isSome then IO.eprintln "loop: LCB's evaluator returns"; return 1
    unless ← writeBackends dir target loopBody (f, tbl) do return 1
    writeExpected dir "incomplete" ""
    return 0

/-- One site, and a body returning `256 + 1`: past the table, so the word encodes nothing. -/
def emitInvalid (dir : System.FilePath) (target : LCB.Target) : IO UInt32 := do
  let tbl : SiteTable := [Examples.plusSite]
  IO.FS.createDirAll dir
  IO.FS.writeFile (dir / "llvm.ll")
    ("define i32 @concrete_body() {\nentry:\n  ret i32 257\n}\n\n" ++ Runtime.llvm tbl)
  let f : LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source.Function :=
    { resultWidth := .word, parameters := #[], valueWidths := #[],
      blocks := #[{ phis := #[], instructions := #[], terminator := .ret (.literal (LCB.word 257)) }] }
  unless ← writeLcb dir target (f, tbl) do return 1
  writeExpected dir "134" (Runtime.invalidLine ++ "\n")
  return 0

def withTarget (name : String) (k : LCB.Target → IO UInt32) : IO UInt32 :=
  match LCB.Target.ofName name with
  | some t => k t
  | none => do IO.eprintln s!"unknown target {name}"; return 2

def main (args : List String) : IO UInt32 := do
  match args with
  | ["list"] => do
    for (n, _, _) in Examples.all do IO.println n
    return 0
  | ["emit", name, dir, t] => withTarget t (emit name dir)
  | ["loop", dir, t] => withTarget t (emitLoop dir)
- `adapt_admitted : compile T src = .ok out → LCB.admit (LCB.adapt out.bir) = .ok _`. It is separate from semantic preservation and uses LCB's own admission function, so it covers every profile check: SSA and dominance validation, reachability of all blocks, exact widths, supported operations only, at most 64 blocks, 480 definitions and the frame bound. It is proved, or, until then, checked for each compilation by `decide` in the certificate.
- `lower_emits_check`: every MIR add without a valid discharged-evidence token is followed by a branch on its flag to a trap site of kind `arithmeticOverflow`, with that add's origin.
- `denotes_behaviour_det`: two denotations of the same `src` have the same `Core.behaviour`. This is stated over behaviour, not at a fixed fuel.

**Program correctness transports**, through the acceptance witness `answer_accepts : compile T₀ answerSrc = .ok answerOut`:

```lean
theorem answer_correct (F : FrontendPremises) (P : NativePremises B N T₀)
    (he : B.emit answerOut.bir = .ok t) (hb : N.Built T₀ t img) : (∃ β, N.Runs T₀ img β) ∧ ∀ β, N.Runs T₀ img β → β = .exited 42
```

**Non-vacuity, one witness per layer:**
1. **Acceptance:** `answer_accepts`, by kernel `decide`.
2. **Native model consistency, per backend.** For LLVM, `NativePremises (llvm refMeans) refNative T₀` is proved for a reference model:
   - `Image := BIR.Program`;
   - `Built T ll b := b.WF ∧ b.frameSlots ≤ T.maxSlots ∧ LLVM.print b = ll`;
   - `BodyRuns T b r := r = BIR.bodyResult b`, where `bodyResult` returns `encode (behaviour b)` or diverges;
   - `Runs T b β := β = BIR.behaviour b`;
   - `refMeans ll r := ∃ b, b.WF ∧ LLVM.print b = ll ∧ r = BIR.bodyResult b`.

   The `rt` field follows from `decode_encode`. The `emit` field needs **printer injectivity**, which is proved: `LLVM.unprint (LLVM.print b) = some b` for every WF `b`. That also makes the `.ll` text determine its BIR, which the certificate uses.

   For LCB, `Means` is fixed by LCB's semantics. The witness `NativePremises lcb refNativeLCB T₀` uses:
   - `Image := LCB.Source.Function × SiteTable`, which is the emitted text itself;
   - `Built T t img := img = t ∧ frame bound`;
   - `BodyRuns T (g, _) := LCB.bodyResult g`;
   - `Runs T (g, tbl) β := ∃ r, LCB.bodyResult g r ∧ β = r.behaviour tbl`.

   Because a successful `emit b` is `(f, b.sites)` (`Proof.emit_ok`), a built image carries exactly `b.sites`, so `rt` holds by definition and no adapter injectivity is needed. `emit` is `emit_correct`.

   `live` holds because LCB's step relation is deterministic, so a run returns, faults or diverges. The witness follows from `adapter_correct` and lands with it.
3. **Frontend premises:** these are claims about fixed code, not something a model can satisfy, so no model witness applies. They are discharged per compilation (next section), and universally once `lex_correct` and `parse_correct` land.
4. **Native-build existence:** this is attested, not proved. The conformance job records toolchain provenance and the image digest for each example.

## Per-compilation certificate (`Concrete/Cert.lean`), R-0552 first increment

The driver emits `Cert_<digest>.lean`, which the kernel checks (`lake env lean`, no `native_decide`):

```lean
def srcBytes : ByteArray := ...      -- the exact input
def expected : Output := ...         -- the exact toks, ast, q, sites, tokens, mir, mem, bir, ll, lcb
-- one entry per edge, each checked by `decide` on that ONE stage function:
theorem e_lex   : Lex.lex srcBytes = .ok expected.toks := by decide
theorem e_parse : Parse.parse expected.toks = .ok expected.ast := by decide
-- … e_elab, e_admit, e_lower, e_memory, e_ssa, e_llvm, e_lcb
theorem cert_compile : compile T₀ srcBytes = .ok expected :=
  compile_of_edges e_lex e_parse e_elab e_admit e_lower e_memory e_ssa e_llvm e_lcb
    g_frame g_lcbAdmit
-- the guards compile also checks, as explicit witnesses:
--   g_frame    : expected.bir.frameSlots ≤ T₀.maxSlots := by decide
--   g_lcbAdmit : LCB.admit expected.lcb = .ok _ := by decide
   Accepted examples `return 125;` and `return 134;` are in the corpus and must classify as exits. Slice 0 can't produce a real startup refusal, because it has no grants. The refusal and trap classifications are also exercised by comparator self-tests: stub images, and the trap via gate 6.

   **Refused examples have their own leg.** The Lean `check` result, the compiler's refusal (with its rule) and the refusal certificate must agree with `.expect`. No executable exists for a refused example. A separate supported-subset coverage report lists every grammar production and Core form that the corpus exercises. A reject-all compiler fails, because every accepted example must exit with its status.
5. **Differential at the source:** generated accepted programs, with replayable seeds; native exit = Lean `run`.
6. **Differential through the token-free lowering, both backends.** This covers overflow. Generated **well-scoped, unchecked** slice-0 programs over **arbitrary** `u8` operands, including `200 + 56`, take `lower q ∅ → memory → ssa`, so they have no tokens and no checker. Their Core `run` is compared with the LLVM path and the LCB path. Overflow must give exit 134 and the right site's trap line on fd 2, for both backends. This exercises token-aware lowering, Memory CFG and SSA, not only the backends.
   A second leg compares generated BIR directly with both backends, to cover BIR shapes that lowering does not produce.

   An in-process leg also compares `BIR.behaviour b` with LCB's own evaluator on the emitted body, an executable cross-check of `emit_correct`.
6a. **AArch64:** a GitHub-hosted AArch64 macOS job (`macos-15`) builds the LCB output for `aarch64-macos`, runs gates 4 and 6 there natively, and compares against Lean. LLVM-native also runs there, as a third cross-check. Gates 4 and 6 for x86-64 run on concrete-ci-2.
7. **Certificates:** the kernel checks every acceptance and refusal certificate, with measured budgets.
8. **Reproducibility** (implemented, `scripts/checks/slice0_repro.sh`): two independent builds give byte-identical outputs at every stage, on each target, and two independent Linux runners give identical manifests. See [Reproducible builds](#reproducible-builds-owner-2026-11-07).
9. **Trusted base** (owner, 2026-11-07): a golden test that `concrete facts` prints exactly the spec's premise list, so a premise added, removed or reworded anywhere fails until the golden is updated with it. Implemented provisionally in `scripts/checks/slice0_trusted_base.sh`: until `concrete facts` exists, `scripts/checks/slice0_trusted_base.lean` prints `Premises.report`: the premise registry `Concrete/Premises.lean`, the sorted axioms of each headline theorem (`Premises.theorems`) and each axiom's type; the gate appends the Lean toolchain and the Lake pins, and the result must equal `tests/golden/slice0_trusted_base.txt` byte for byte. The README's open-premise table must equal the registry's rendering (`Premises.tableRow`). Controls cover the comparison, a failing printer, the axiom collection and a drifted table. The statements are text until `FrontendPremises` and `NativePremises` land as Lean structures; each entry then names its field.
10. **Sanitizers** (owner, 2026-11-07; UBSan dropped by the owner, 2026-11-08; implemented in `scripts/checks/slice0_artifacts.sh`): every LLVM example also runs built with ASan in CI, and must give the same observation with no sanitizer report. ASan instruments only functions marked `sanitize_address`, which clang's C front end adds and the printed IR lacks, so the sanitizer build adds it to every definition (`slice0_native.sh sanitize`); a control body with an out-of-bounds load must be reported. There is no UBSan leg: UBSan's checks are inserted by clang's C front end, so on LLVM IR input it checks nothing. No C is compiled in slice 0, and the LLVM-level undefined behaviour of the printed IR (poison, `unreachable`) stays P_print's.
11. **Binary size** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented, same script): the size of each example's executable (bytes and text size, pinned `llvm-size`), per backend and target, is recorded per run in `sizes.tsv`, uploaded with the job. Report-only: it never fails a run. **Compile time** (owner, 2026-11-08; same script, report-only): each example's emit step (the Lean emitter, Lean start-up included) and native step (clang and LCB's command line) in wall-clock milliseconds, next to its budget (the step's time bound), in `compile_times.tsv`.
12. **Stack bound** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented in the same script and `lib/stack_bound.py`; the `aarch64-macos` run check with LLDB, 2026-11-08): a stack-bound fact for each example, derived from the final artifact (the linked executable, not the IR) for both backends, and checked against a run. The analysis follows every path of the Concrete-owned code, instruction by instruction from an explicit set of modelled instructions, (`main`, `concrete_rt_write_all`, `concrete_body`) over the disassembly's control-flow graph, tracking the stack and frame pointers' depth below `main`'s entry and every byte addressed relative to them (so the red zone), and fails closed on anything it does not model (an instruction outside the set, another stack-pointer write, frame- or link-register writeback, an indirect branch or call, a stack pointer copied out, an indexed stack address, a stack read of bytes the function has not itself written on every path since its last call (so no stale saved frame pointer is ever loaded), a saved slot read other than by the modelled restore, two depths meeting, recursion, a call to a function neither owned nor a PLT/stub). The external calls (`write`, `_exit`, `abort`) are listed with the depth at their entry; their own use, and that `_exit` and `abort` do not return, are the libc premise. On x86-64 Linux the run check single-steps the owned code under the pinned gdb and requires the measured stack-pointer depth to be within the bound, for a complete run only (stopped first at `main`, back at each external call's return address, no signal, the expected exit status). On `aarch64-macos` the run check is the same, under Apple's LLDB (`/usr/bin/lldb`, `lib/stack_measure_lldb.py`; an external call's return address is the link register); LLDB is not pinned (Apple's signed debugserver is the only one allowed to attach), so its version is printed in the gate log. `stack.tsv` records bound, measurement and externals.

With the front end (owner, 2026-11-07): gate 5's random-program fuzzer compares the native executables with the Lean evaluator, and a JSON schema generated from the Lean types of the `facts` output has goldens. Each gate joins `required-checks.txt` when the compiler lands.

**Negative controls**, each a mutation the matching gate must refuse:
- a deleted pass theorem;
- an unrelated theorem substituted as a stage's evidence;
- a silently omitted stage;
- an extra or strengthened premise;
- a changed premise-definition digest;
- a certificate for the wrong source, BIR, `.ll` or LCB-IR;
- a swapped executable or toolchain;
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Slice-0 reproducibility gate (Concrete/README.md, gate 8). Two independent builds of the compiler
# and of LCB, from two source copies with different paths and TMPDIRs, emit every hand-written BIR
# example and build it natively; every stage is compared byte for byte, with no normalization.
# The two sides share only the pinned Lean toolchain and the dependency SOURCES (each side compiles
# them itself, with no Lake artifact cache). The comparator is checked by negative controls: a flipped byte, a missing artifact
# and a differing inventory must each be refused.
#
#   slice0_repro.sh [MANIFEST]   (inside nix develop, from the repository root)
#
# MANIFEST gets the build identity and one hash per artifact; CI compares the manifests of two
# independent runners of the same target.
set -euo pipefail
root=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/../.." && pwd)
cd "$root"
# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/limits.sh
source scripts/checks/lib/limits.sh
# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/slice0_sources.sh
source scripts/checks/lib/slice0_sources.sh
gate_limits 64 40000
work=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "${work:?}"' EXIT
BOUNDED_PARENT="$work"
# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh
source scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh
[ "$BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG" = 1 ] || { echo "slice0_repro: python3 is required for bounded runs" >&2; exit 2; }
# bounded SECS CMD...: CMD through bounded_run (stdin /dev/null, one re-run on a timeout; a second
# timeout returns 124 with no output and is reported INCOMPLETE, never a pass).
bounded() {
  local secs=$1 rc=0
  shift
  bounded_run -i /dev/null "$secs" -- "$@" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" != 0 ] && bounded_is_incomplete "$rc"; then
    echo "slice0_repro: INCOMPLETE (timed out twice): $*" >&2
  fi
  return "$rc"
}
manifest=${1:-}
[ -z "$manifest" ] || manifest=$(realpath -m "$manifest")
compare=(python3 "$root/scripts/checks/lib/repro_compare.py")
# Materialize the dependency checkouts (no build): they must be the manifest's clean revisions,
# which build_identity.sh checks before anything is built.
bounded 600 lake env true
bash scripts/checks/build_identity.sh > "$work/identity"
target=$(bash scripts/checks/slice0_native.sh target)
# Working source edits are included (the identity records their digest); no build product is.
for side in first second; do
  d="$work/$side/checkout-with-$side-path"
  mkdir -p "$d/.lake/packages" "$work/$side/tmp"
  git ls-files -z -- "${slice0_sources[@]}" |
    tar --null -T - -cf - | tar -xf - -C "$d"
  # Dependency checkouts: tracked files and .git (which Lake checks against the manifest) only.
  for pkg in .lake/packages/*/; do
    pkg=$(basename "$pkg")
    mkdir -p "$d/.lake/packages/$pkg"
    (cd ".lake/packages/$pkg" && { git ls-files -z; printf '.git\0'; } | tar --null -T - -cf -) |
      tar -xf - -C "$d/.lake/packages/$pkg"
  done
  (
    cd "$d"
    export TMPDIR="$work/$side/tmp" LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1 ZERO_AR_DATE=1
    # No Lake artifact cache: each side compiles everything it uses.
    export LAKE_ARTIFACT_CACHE=false LAKE_CACHE_DIR="$work/$side/lake-cache"
    bounded 3000 lake build Concrete lean-compiler-backend/lcb >"$work/$side/build.log" 2>&1 ||
      { tail -50 "$work/$side/build.log" >&2; echo "slice0_repro: $side: build failed" >&2; exit 1; }
    lcb="$d/.lake/packages/lean-compiler-backend/.lake/build/bin/lcb"
    bounded 120 lake env lean --run scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean list > names
    [ -s names ] || { echo 'slice0_repro: no examples' >&2; exit 1; }
    while read -r name; do
      bounded 120 lake env lean --run scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean emit "$name" "$d/out/$name" "$target"
      bounded 120 bash scripts/checks/slice0_native.sh "$d/out/$name" "$lcb"
    done < names
  )
done
a="$work/first/checkout-with-first-path"
b="$work/second/checkout-with-second-path"
"${compare[@]}" "$a" "$b" "$work/identity" ${manifest:+"$manifest"}

# Negative controls: the comparator must refuse each perturbation of an identical pair.
control() { # control LABEL EXPECTED-MESSAGE SETUP...
  local label=$1 expect=$2 c="$work/ctl/$1" rc=0; shift 2
  mkdir -p "$c"
  cp -R "$a" "$c/a"; cp -R "$a" "$c/b"
  (cd "$c/b" && "$@")
  "${compare[@]}" "$c/a" "$c/b" "$work/identity" >"$c/log" 2>&1 || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" = 0 ] || ! grep -qF -- "$expect" "$c/log"; then
    echo "FAIL slice0_repro control $label: not refused as '$expect'" >&2; cat "$c/log" >&2; exit 1
  fi
  echo "ok slice0_repro control $label: refused"
}
first=$(head -1 "$a/names")
flip() { python3 -c 'import sys;p=sys.argv[1];d=bytearray(open(p,"rb").read());d[len(d)//2]^=1;open(p,"wb").write(d)' "$1"; }
control flipped-byte "$first: lcb.exe:" flip "out/$first/lcb.exe"
control missing-artifact "$first: lcb_rt.o: missing" rm "out/$first/lcb_rt.o"
control inventory 'example inventory differs' sh -c 'echo extra >> names'
echo 'PASS slice0_repro'
scripts/checks/slice0_repro.sh:# bounded SECS CMD...: CMD through bounded_run (stdin /dev/null, one re-run on a timeout; a second
scripts/checks/slice0_repro.sh:# timeout returns 124 with no output and is reported INCOMPLETE, never a pass).
scripts/checks/lib/slice0_common.sh:# bounded SECS CMD...: CMD under `capped`, through bounded_run (stdin /dev/null; a timeout is
scripts/checks/lib/slice0_common.sh:# re-run once with a larger budget; a second timeout writes no output, returns 124 and is reported
scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean:  if let .error msg := LCB.assemble .aarch64Macos f then
scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean:  match LCB.assemble target f with
scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean:  match LCB.emit p with
scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean:  match LCB.emit loopBody with
Concrete/README.md:The **BIR → LCB-IR adapter** is the first backend proof target. `adapter_correct` states that the adapter's output, under **LCB's own semantics** (`LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source`, the `ssa-int-v2` profile, imported and never restated), has the BIR behaviour. It is proved (`Proof.adapter_correct`) for every WF program the adapter accepts (`adapt p = .ok f`). The backend contract is complete (owner, 2026-11-08): `LCB.emit : Program → Except String (Source.Function × SiteTable)` represents adaptation failure, `LCB.artifact` builds only from a successful `emit`, and `Proof.emit_correct` (success implies correctness), `Proof.artifact_of_emit_error` (a refusal produces no artifact, for every target) and `Proof.artifact_correct` (every artifact comes from a correct `emit`) state it. `P_adapter` is retired; the trusted-base golden (gate 9) records it.
Concrete/README.md:def lcb : Backend := ⟨LCB.Source.Function × SiteTable, LCB.emit,
Concrete/README.md:   - `out.tokens = admit out.p`, `out.mir = lower out.p out.tokens`, `out.mem = memory out.mir`, `out.bir = ssa out.mem`, `out.ll = LLVM.print out.bir` and `out.lcb = LCB.adapt out.bir`;
Concrete/README.md:- `adapter_correct : b.WF → LCB.adapt b = .ok f → LCB.bodyResult f r → r.behaviour b.sites = BIR.behaviour b` (proved, `Backend/LCB.lean`), over all WF BIR the adapter accepts, overflow included, against LCB's **raw** `Source.Function` semantics (`LeanCompilerBackend/Verified/Source.lean`, imported, never restated). `emit_correct` lifts it to `LCB.emit` and fills `NativePremises lcb`'s `emit` field.
Concrete/README.md:- `adapt_admitted : compile T src = .ok out → LCB.admit (LCB.adapt out.bir) = .ok _`. It is separate from semantic preservation and uses LCB's own admission function, so it covers every profile check: SSA and dominance validation, reachability of all blocks, exact widths, supported operations only, at most 64 blocks, 480 definitions and the frame bound. It is proved, or, until then, checked for each compilation by `decide` in the certificate.
Concrete/README.md:`timeout -k` on every build and emitter call. Every executable under test runs under `bounded_run`

